00:02:09.531 --> 00:02:11.003
But now without further ado,

00:02:11.003 --> 00:02:12.515
let's jump into the session

00:02:12.515 --> 00:02:13.348
In my head,

00:02:13.348 --> 00:02:15.367
I think about four different
types of internships

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:16.377
that you can do.

00:02:16.377 --> 00:02:18.952
So corporate, consulting,
banking, or startup.

00:02:18.952 --> 00:02:20.809
So from a business perspective for me,

00:02:20.809 --> 00:02:22.871
these are the four categories,

00:02:22.871 --> 00:02:25.017
and I'd believe that either of them

00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:26.003
in theory works well.

00:02:26.003 --> 00:02:28.032
I believe that what consultant firms,

00:02:28.032 --> 00:02:29.674
of course like to see us,

00:02:29.674 --> 00:02:31.557
you having done some kind of experience

00:02:31.557 --> 00:02:32.946
in consulting before.

00:02:32.946 --> 00:02:34.555
So, especially all I could target

00:02:34.555 --> 00:02:37.147
is to get into McKinsey or BCG or Bain.

00:02:37.147 --> 00:02:38.485
One of the MBB firms,

00:02:38.485 --> 00:02:40.609
then probably having done some
internships at one of the,

00:02:40.609 --> 00:02:43.334
maybe to two or one of the
smaller consulting firms,

00:02:43.334 --> 00:02:48.334
maybe, Willem Baga or the Big
Four firms, PWC, Deloitte,

00:02:48.704 --> 00:02:50.515
Ernst &amp; Young, KPMG.

00:02:50.515 --> 00:02:53.213
So these kind of a bit of smaller tiers

00:02:53.213 --> 00:02:54.387
would be great internships to do,

00:02:54.387 --> 00:02:55.853
to prepare yourself,

00:02:55.853 --> 00:02:58.026
but you do not necessarily
need to do that.

00:02:58.026 --> 00:03:01.188
Another category that is
often also very regarded is

00:03:01.188 --> 00:03:03.142
internships in corporate firms.

00:03:03.142 --> 00:03:04.002
And especially corporate firms

00:03:04.002 --> 00:03:06.276
that our potential clients
of confiding firms, right?

00:03:06.276 --> 00:03:09.964
So the big blue-chip companies,
especially in Munich,

00:03:09.964 --> 00:03:11.869
you're lucky that there are so many,

00:03:11.869 --> 00:03:14.452
no ducks, 30 companies in Munich,

00:03:14.452 --> 00:03:15.848
Zeeman's and so on.

00:03:15.848 --> 00:03:17.078
So these are companies that

00:03:17.078 --> 00:03:19.167
are clients of consulting firms,

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:21.155
and consulting firms love that.

00:03:21.155 --> 00:03:22.644
If you have intern that as well,

00:03:22.644 --> 00:03:24.227
because this shows them that

00:03:24.227 --> 00:03:26.290
you have already seen the
client side of the whole thing.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.015
You have already have an understanding

00:03:28.015 --> 00:03:31.133
how it looks like to be
at these kinds of firms,

00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:33.265
the insight that these
people who work there.

00:03:33.265 --> 00:03:34.344
So here as the well,

00:03:34.344 --> 00:03:36.057
I would encourage you there as well.

00:03:36.057 --> 00:03:37.430
And especially, again, if you're a Munich,

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:38.889
you have the best opportunities

00:03:38.889 --> 00:03:42.943
because you just have all these
great household names just,

00:03:42.943 --> 00:03:44.317
after behind your door,

00:03:44.317 --> 00:03:45.694
it's just perfect.

00:03:45.694 --> 00:03:48.043
And yes, just doing some
entrepreneurial things.

00:03:48.043 --> 00:03:50.708
Other work achievements are
interesting here as well.

00:03:50.708 --> 00:03:52.962
So if you have a little
entrepreneurial adventure,

00:03:52.962 --> 00:03:55.763
maybe that you build up during
university with your friends,

00:03:55.763 --> 00:03:57.095
this just shows that,

00:03:57.095 --> 00:03:58.766
you're a bit of an entrepreneurial guy

00:03:58.766 --> 00:04:00.476
who can show outside of the box,

00:04:00.476 --> 00:04:01.309
who can think outside of the box.

00:04:01.309 --> 00:04:05.643
And this is always very well
valued by the consulting firms.

00:04:05.643 --> 00:04:09.999
Yes, consulting firms do
look at extracurriculars.

00:04:09.999 --> 00:04:13.341
They do look at what you
have done left and right.

00:04:13.341 --> 00:04:15.718
And I think the main rationale is that

00:04:15.718 --> 00:04:19.560
for a consulting firm, what
they will expect from people is

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.818
not only that you do your
job well, on the job, right?

00:04:22.818 --> 00:04:24.589
Not only that you do your job well,

00:04:24.589 --> 00:04:26.900
being a consultant on the job,

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.176
but they will also expect you to help out

00:04:29.176 --> 00:04:32.027
in your office to take up
some additional initiatives

00:04:32.027 --> 00:04:33.157
in your office,

00:04:33.157 --> 00:04:35.465
just to come up with
some great ideas as well.

00:04:35.465 --> 00:04:36.306
But then you will do

00:04:36.306 --> 00:04:38.233
on top of all your consulting work, right?

00:04:38.233 --> 00:04:39.876
This will be expected
from you at some point

00:04:39.876 --> 00:04:41.842
after the first year or so,

00:04:41.842 --> 00:04:44.266
that you really take up
responsibility left and right,

00:04:44.266 --> 00:04:46.468
doing some initiative projects,

00:04:46.468 --> 00:04:48.036
helping out in your office

00:04:48.036 --> 00:04:48.869
and that's effective.

00:04:48.869 --> 00:04:50.995
Then you are able to
commit yourself socially

00:04:50.995 --> 00:04:53.701
to some organizations that
you took the initiative

00:04:53.701 --> 00:04:58.225
in the past, maybe politics or
some other force activities.

00:04:58.225 --> 00:05:00.988
This just shows that you
are this type of person

00:05:00.988 --> 00:05:03.337
that tumor just comes naturally

00:05:03.337 --> 00:05:04.608
to do all these kinds of things.

00:05:04.608 --> 00:05:06.960
And which is why
extracurricular experiences here

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.710
is important as well.

00:05:09.595 --> 00:05:10.582
Then honors and awards.

00:05:10.582 --> 00:05:13.627
So this is kind of the
first experience section.

00:05:13.627 --> 00:05:16.290
One thing that I believe for
some people is very present

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:18.489
and for other people
that just do not have it

00:05:18.489 --> 00:05:20.194
in their (mumbles) is scholarships.

00:05:20.194 --> 00:05:23.043
So I think that scholarships
can be very helpful.

00:05:23.043 --> 00:05:24.370
And for two things, right?

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:25.203
But first of course,

00:05:25.203 --> 00:05:26.917
it helps you view your university degrees,

00:05:26.917 --> 00:05:28.172
because you get money

00:05:28.172 --> 00:05:29.242
and also a great network

00:05:29.242 --> 00:05:31.427
if you are in these scholarship programs.

00:05:31.427 --> 00:05:35.030
But it's also very good
signal to consulting firms

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:36.293
because the reason is that,

00:05:36.293 --> 00:05:39.330
especially these BMBF
scholarship programs.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:40.558
So if you don't know it,

00:05:40.558 --> 00:05:44.397
the BMBF is Bundesministerium
für Bildung und Forschung.

00:05:44.397 --> 00:05:47.508
So it's one of the big
ministerium in Germany,

00:05:47.508 --> 00:05:50.308
one of the big government
departments in Germany.

00:05:50.308 --> 00:05:52.121
And they have a scholarship program,

00:05:52.121 --> 00:05:55.294
and they have i think, 12
or 13 different institutions

00:05:55.294 --> 00:05:57.829
that give out the scholarships,

00:05:57.829 --> 00:05:59.102
and probably the most well known is

00:05:59.102 --> 00:06:02.057
the Studienstiftung des deutschen Volkes.

00:06:02.057 --> 00:06:04.357
But then there are also
all kinds of scholarships.

00:06:04.357 --> 00:06:06.942
Each political party has
one of the institution,

00:06:06.942 --> 00:06:09.652
but then there's also the
Stiftung der deutschen Wirtschaft,

00:06:09.652 --> 00:06:11.616
all the big religions have an institution

00:06:11.616 --> 00:06:13.794
to which they give scholarships and so on.

00:06:13.794 --> 00:06:15.437
So just the researchers, if
you're not familiar with that,

00:06:15.437 --> 00:06:17.461
it's a very great opportunity.

00:06:17.461 --> 00:06:19.964
And at least as far as I know,

00:06:19.964 --> 00:06:22.104
you do not necessarily
need to be a German citizen

00:06:22.104 --> 00:06:23.508
to get the money,

00:06:23.508 --> 00:06:24.574
to do your own research here,

00:06:24.574 --> 00:06:26.491
but it's a great opportunity
both to get funding.

00:06:26.491 --> 00:06:29.066
And it is a really high regarded,

00:06:29.066 --> 00:06:31.184
is highly seen by German employer's

00:06:31.184 --> 00:06:34.260
because it shows them
that you were already able

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:38.427
to go through a pretty
advanced recruiting process

00:06:39.418 --> 00:06:41.211
or a pretty advanced selection process

00:06:41.211 --> 00:06:43.422
due to the sole scholarship thing,

00:06:43.422 --> 00:06:45.600
because often they have been a
weekend off assessment center

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:46.966
or something like that,
that you need to go through

00:06:46.966 --> 00:06:48.676
in order to get admitted to that.

00:06:48.676 --> 00:06:50.819
So that really shows that

00:06:50.819 --> 00:06:51.932
you're just up to the task, right?

00:06:51.932 --> 00:06:55.382
And if they know invite you
to their interview process,

00:06:55.382 --> 00:06:57.250
they have some confidence

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.162
that you were already able to pass

00:06:59.162 --> 00:07:02.096
such a difficult process in the past.

00:07:02.096 --> 00:07:04.351
And at McKinsey, at least

00:07:04.351 --> 00:07:07.589
I'd say that 60, 70%
of the people probably

00:07:07.589 --> 00:07:10.109
had one of these BMBF scholarships.

00:07:10.109 --> 00:07:11.370
So it's pretty common.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:12.579
Multiple people have it,

00:07:12.579 --> 00:07:14.035
and I really encourage you to get it.

00:07:14.035 --> 00:07:17.418
Also for me, it was a great experience.

00:07:17.418 --> 00:07:20.025
Besides that, of course, Dean's
list, other academic honors,

00:07:20.025 --> 00:07:21.743
try to collect this stuff as well.

00:07:21.743 --> 00:07:23.414
And if you have any prices, of course,

00:07:23.414 --> 00:07:25.558
this is always where we got it, right?

00:07:25.558 --> 00:07:26.391
And this now of course

00:07:26.391 --> 00:07:28.947
is then also the content
that then you should try

00:07:28.947 --> 00:07:29.835
to get (mumbles) CV

00:07:29.835 --> 00:07:32.532
because this is the stuff
that will make you interesting

00:07:32.532 --> 00:07:35.865
for these big strategy consulting firms.

00:07:36.778 --> 00:07:38.603
I'm sure you have lots
of questions on that.

00:07:38.603 --> 00:07:41.281
So I am sure that later we
can also dive into that.

00:07:41.281 --> 00:07:43.352
Let me just quickly go
over the presentation.

00:07:43.352 --> 00:07:44.882
First of all, because now we are going

00:07:44.882 --> 00:07:45.806
to the second element,

00:07:45.806 --> 00:07:48.238
and this is now the written application,

00:07:48.238 --> 00:07:51.257
and this pretty much consists
of two main documents,

00:07:51.257 --> 00:07:53.758
namely the cover letter and the CV.

00:07:53.758 --> 00:07:55.035
And the cover letter,

00:07:55.035 --> 00:07:58.267
yeah, I know that many people
therese out about a lot,

00:07:58.267 --> 00:07:59.100
oh, my dear,

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:00.993
what am I writing in
this cover letter, right?

00:08:00.993 --> 00:08:02.532
And I believe the truth is that

00:08:02.532 --> 00:08:05.659
there's is pretty much
seen as a hygiene factor.

00:08:05.659 --> 00:08:07.350
And this means that,

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.553
having a lego, super great cover letter,

00:08:09.553 --> 00:08:11.143
this is not what will
get you the job, right?

00:08:11.143 --> 00:08:12.861
I mean, nobody is going to say, "Oh wow,

00:08:12.861 --> 00:08:15.860
this guy, has just such
an amazing cover letter."

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:19.081
But of course, if you just mess up here,

00:08:19.081 --> 00:08:20.903
if you do something that's unprofessional,

00:08:20.903 --> 00:08:23.867
then this would just
be seen as a big no-go.

00:08:23.867 --> 00:08:25.717
And then of course you,

00:08:25.717 --> 00:08:27.267
so it's important just to get it right,

00:08:27.267 --> 00:08:28.408
but don't believe that

00:08:28.408 --> 00:08:29.763
this is now the most important thing

00:08:29.763 --> 00:08:31.853
in this whole application.

00:08:31.853 --> 00:08:33.172
And just kind of three suggestions

00:08:33.172 --> 00:08:34.172
that I wanna give you,

00:08:34.172 --> 00:08:35.326
three tips that I wanna give you

00:08:35.326 --> 00:08:37.435
for the cover letter.

00:08:37.435 --> 00:08:40.090
First, and this is now
here the third bullet,

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:42.195
make sure that you're very clearly,

00:08:42.195 --> 00:08:43.929
probably in the first paragraph

00:08:43.929 --> 00:08:45.244
of your cover letter,

00:08:45.244 --> 00:08:47.790
that you communicate about three things.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:50.271
First, when you want to start,

00:08:50.271 --> 00:08:52.641
Second, in which office you want to start,

00:08:52.641 --> 00:08:55.605
and third, what is the
specific role you want to in.

00:08:55.605 --> 00:08:56.690
I mean, this for me,

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:58.850
I like the most important
point of information

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:00.671
that should be included
in the cover letter.

00:09:00.671 --> 00:09:02.676
But I like to read lots of cover letters,

00:09:02.676 --> 00:09:03.978
so friends also send me them,

00:09:03.978 --> 00:09:05.348
but this is not included.

00:09:05.348 --> 00:09:07.739
So make sure that this kind
of (mumbles) is included

00:09:07.739 --> 00:09:09.599
just as a basic points of information

00:09:09.599 --> 00:09:10.758
that should be in there.

00:09:10.758 --> 00:09:12.972
And then also answer two things,

00:09:12.972 --> 00:09:13.805
namely first,

00:09:13.805 --> 00:09:15.252
why do you want to work in consulting?

00:09:15.252 --> 00:09:16.592
What is your motivation?

00:09:16.592 --> 00:09:17.494
And then finally,

00:09:17.494 --> 00:09:18.816
like, why this firm?

00:09:18.816 --> 00:09:21.164
I mean, what entices
you to work at McKinsey

00:09:21.164 --> 00:09:22.298
or at BCG and so on.

00:09:22.298 --> 00:09:24.081
And I know this can be difficult

00:09:24.081 --> 00:09:25.772
because I mean, these firms,

00:09:25.772 --> 00:09:28.347
they're are not that
difficult if you compare them,

00:09:28.347 --> 00:09:29.915
but I think it is always something right

00:09:29.915 --> 00:09:30.901
that you can draw on

00:09:30.901 --> 00:09:33.215
is maybe if you've been
to a recruiting event,

00:09:33.215 --> 00:09:35.122
and you really enjoyed the people there,

00:09:35.122 --> 00:09:36.786
if you maybe know a
name of a recruiter also

00:09:36.786 --> 00:09:38.051
that you work too.

00:09:38.051 --> 00:09:39.694
And I mean, now you know me, right?

00:09:39.694 --> 00:09:40.527
So if you want to apply to McKinsey,

00:09:40.527 --> 00:09:42.552
you can also say that I talked to him.

00:09:42.552 --> 00:09:43.385
And alumnus of the firm,

00:09:43.385 --> 00:09:45.125
and you just really liked it, right?

00:09:45.125 --> 00:09:47.004
So, I mean, just pointing out
some personal relationships

00:09:47.004 --> 00:09:47.837
that you have

00:09:47.837 --> 00:09:49.253
and just pointing out

00:09:49.253 --> 00:09:50.715
that due to these personal relationships,

00:09:50.715 --> 00:09:52.897
this is what really believes
that you like the people,

00:09:52.897 --> 00:09:53.778
that you like the style,

00:09:53.778 --> 00:09:55.034
that you like the culture,

00:09:55.034 --> 00:09:57.967
this is just one of the
point that you could draw on

00:09:57.967 --> 00:10:01.599
if you talk about
choosing a specific firm.

00:10:01.599 --> 00:10:03.624
Few thoughts on the CV.

00:10:03.624 --> 00:10:04.899
So here, I know that in Germany

00:10:04.899 --> 00:10:07.158
many people like the two-page CV format,

00:10:07.158 --> 00:10:09.175
I believe using two pages
for those of you ...

00:10:09.175 --> 00:10:10.478
It's also okay

00:10:10.478 --> 00:10:11.402
for sure,

00:10:11.402 --> 00:10:13.219
do not use more than three pages.

00:10:13.219 --> 00:10:14.583
I mean, this would be very bad.

00:10:14.583 --> 00:10:16.290
Just try to stick to two.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.853
And personally, I believe
that the one page format

00:10:18.853 --> 00:10:19.834
is the way to go.

00:10:19.834 --> 00:10:20.842
It's the best format.

00:10:20.842 --> 00:10:21.675
It's just so amazing

00:10:21.675 --> 00:10:25.771
just to have everything
on one piece of paper,

00:10:25.771 --> 00:10:26.604
'cause of grasp

00:10:26.604 --> 00:10:27.567
it's so much better.

00:10:27.567 --> 00:10:28.400
I just love that.

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:30.008
And I'm sure that the HR departments

00:10:30.008 --> 00:10:30.885
will have that as well.

00:10:30.885 --> 00:10:34.491
So whenever possible, try to
get it down on one page CV.

00:10:34.491 --> 00:10:35.324
And here you know,

00:10:35.324 --> 00:10:36.493
I know many people that are saying,

00:10:36.493 --> 00:10:39.763
Oh, Heinrich, I'm just such a great guy.

00:10:39.763 --> 00:10:41.945
I just put so much stuff in my life.

00:10:41.945 --> 00:10:44.878
No way I'm going to be able
to fit that on one page.

00:10:44.878 --> 00:10:45.711
And I mean,

00:10:45.711 --> 00:10:47.119
this is just complete BS, right?

00:10:47.119 --> 00:10:47.952
I mean,

00:10:47.952 --> 00:10:50.705
even if you are like a
CEO of a Duck's company

00:10:50.705 --> 00:10:52.406
with 30 years of work experience,

00:10:52.406 --> 00:10:54.846
you will always be able to
put it on a one page CV.

00:10:54.846 --> 00:10:57.753
So just push yourself
out there to do that.

00:10:57.753 --> 00:10:58.586
Regarding the CV,

00:10:58.586 --> 00:10:59.703
I think the key sections

00:10:59.703 --> 00:11:01.449
should be pretty much what we discussed

00:11:01.449 --> 00:11:02.282
in the last page.

00:11:02.282 --> 00:11:04.222
So Education, Work experience,
Extracurriculars, Honors

00:11:04.222 --> 00:11:05.055
and awards

00:11:05.055 --> 00:11:07.147
and then also other qualifications.

00:11:07.147 --> 00:11:08.308
So these should be kind
of the main chapters

00:11:08.308 --> 00:11:09.389
that you then have there

00:11:09.389 --> 00:11:11.236
and that you then discuss.

00:11:11.236 --> 00:11:13.676
And there's some more general remarks

00:11:13.676 --> 00:11:15.557
that I see often made wrong in CV's

00:11:15.557 --> 00:11:19.557
is regarding the highlighting
your contribution.

00:11:20.715 --> 00:11:23.885
And often people use formulation such as,

00:11:23.885 --> 00:11:27.148
we did something, our team
did this, we did something

00:11:27.148 --> 00:11:29.862
in the CVs and the bullets that you write

00:11:29.862 --> 00:11:31.023
on your CV,

00:11:31.023 --> 00:11:33.750
make sure that you very much focused on

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:34.992
what you did,

00:11:34.992 --> 00:11:36.350
what your impact was,

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.014
what your contribution was,

00:11:38.014 --> 00:11:40.054
what did you achieve in a specific job

00:11:40.054 --> 00:11:41.553
in the last internship?

00:11:41.553 --> 00:11:43.235
What did you do?

00:11:43.235 --> 00:11:45.668
What was your contribution?

00:11:45.668 --> 00:11:46.969
And whenever possible,

00:11:46.969 --> 00:11:48.837
also quantify that,

00:11:48.837 --> 00:11:50.567
whenever possible, put a number to it,

00:11:50.567 --> 00:11:54.091
to really make sure
that it's very tangible

00:11:54.091 --> 00:11:58.271
what your achievements
of your impact actually.

00:11:58.271 --> 00:12:00.826
And now, because I think now
I'm already over the time

00:12:00.826 --> 00:12:02.691
of the 50 minutes that
I was supposed to talk

00:12:02.691 --> 00:12:04.724
just a few quick thoughts on,

00:12:04.724 --> 00:12:06.873
now the interview process,

00:12:06.873 --> 00:12:08.954
so the third key building
block of getting in,

00:12:08.954 --> 00:12:10.777
so usually there's three aspects of that

00:12:10.777 --> 00:12:12.591
you have the CV interview,

00:12:12.591 --> 00:12:14.491
you have the case interview

00:12:14.491 --> 00:12:16.374
and you have an experience interview.

00:12:16.374 --> 00:12:17.637
And sometimes this can be a bit mixed

00:12:17.637 --> 00:12:18.779
depending on the firm.

00:12:18.779 --> 00:12:20.395
Sometimes you will have an interview

00:12:20.395 --> 00:12:22.482
where both the CV interview
and the case interview

00:12:22.482 --> 00:12:24.136
or interview, where you have the case

00:12:24.136 --> 00:12:26.039
and then an experienced interview,

00:12:26.039 --> 00:12:27.602
but just a few brief point out

00:12:27.602 --> 00:12:29.395
on these three interview types.

00:12:29.395 --> 00:12:31.015
So just first this explanation,

00:12:31.015 --> 00:12:32.325
what is a CV interview?

00:12:32.325 --> 00:12:35.097
A CV interview is where you
basically go over the CV

00:12:35.097 --> 00:12:37.218
with your interviewer, right?

00:12:37.218 --> 00:12:38.627
So the interview,

00:12:38.627 --> 00:12:39.755
you sit together with the interviewer

00:12:39.755 --> 00:12:42.551
and then common questions are things like,

00:12:42.551 --> 00:12:44.639
okay, please introduce yourself

00:12:44.639 --> 00:12:47.129
or please guide me through your CV.

00:12:47.129 --> 00:12:49.145
So these are some very basic questions

00:12:49.145 --> 00:12:51.588
that you will find in almost
every interview process,

00:12:51.588 --> 00:12:52.776
and just make sure

00:12:52.776 --> 00:12:56.498
that you have a little
concise talk prepared, right?

00:12:56.498 --> 00:12:57.822
So if somebody asks you,

00:12:57.822 --> 00:13:01.130
okay, please give me a little
introduction about yourself

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:02.552
then this is now not the first time

00:13:02.552 --> 00:13:03.757
you think about what to say,

00:13:03.757 --> 00:13:05.061
but then you have like a little talk,

00:13:05.061 --> 00:13:06.624
maybe five minutes or so prepared,

00:13:06.624 --> 00:13:09.903
they really focus on the
most important stuff.

00:13:09.903 --> 00:13:11.977
And you're able to give
a little presentation

00:13:11.977 --> 00:13:14.150
about your CV, about your experiences

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:15.490
in five minutes or so

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:17.240
to really guide the interviewer through.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:18.866
I think this is really
the most important thing,

00:13:18.866 --> 00:13:19.996
that you not get lost

00:13:19.996 --> 00:13:22.456
on one of the minor details in your life,

00:13:22.456 --> 00:13:25.059
but are able to give a nice
and concise presentation

00:13:25.059 --> 00:13:25.976
on your CV.

00:13:26.899 --> 00:13:29.494
The second is the case interview.

00:13:29.494 --> 00:13:31.762
And I know this is probably
what most people are afraid of

00:13:31.762 --> 00:13:34.098
when they think about
interviewing with consulting.

00:13:34.098 --> 00:13:35.771
I mean, this is where
people go crazy, right?

00:13:35.771 --> 00:13:37.573
And really don't know what to do.

00:13:37.573 --> 00:13:41.059
And here, just my main
recommendation would be,

00:13:41.059 --> 00:13:43.360
yes, you should read
or you should practice

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:45.847
and yes, you should read all the material

00:13:45.847 --> 00:13:47.083
that's all there in the Internet.

00:13:47.083 --> 00:13:48.318
There are lots of great resources

00:13:48.318 --> 00:13:50.825
where you can prepare for cases.

00:13:50.825 --> 00:13:52.479
What I believe you should not do

00:13:52.479 --> 00:13:55.233
is now to learn 50 frameworks

00:13:55.233 --> 00:13:58.058
for all different subtypes of questions

00:13:58.058 --> 00:13:59.806
that you could be asked.

00:13:59.806 --> 00:14:01.924
I mean, you would just go
crazy if you do that, right?

00:14:01.924 --> 00:14:03.936
So it's not about learning lots and lots

00:14:03.936 --> 00:14:06.155
and lots of frameworks by heart,

00:14:06.155 --> 00:14:08.157
that they need to repeat
in the interview process,

00:14:08.157 --> 00:14:10.999
because this will also not
come across as natural.

00:14:10.999 --> 00:14:13.707
This will not come across as reno.

00:14:13.707 --> 00:14:16.745
You finding an innovative
solution to a problem

00:14:16.745 --> 00:14:19.218
that you were provided
in the case interview,

00:14:19.218 --> 00:14:22.614
but rather what I would
recommend you to do instead is,

00:14:22.614 --> 00:14:25.697
prepare two or three basic frameworks

00:14:26.554 --> 00:14:30.868
that you can use to
qualitatively structure problems,

00:14:30.868 --> 00:14:32.518
structure things.

00:14:32.518 --> 00:14:34.059
And if you just start learning,

00:14:34.059 --> 00:14:35.891
start reading into these frameworks,

00:14:35.891 --> 00:14:36.936
I'm sure that after some time

00:14:36.936 --> 00:14:39.123
you can really distill
it to two or three ones

00:14:39.123 --> 00:14:41.963
that will really helps you to
answer most of the questions.

00:14:41.963 --> 00:14:43.634
Yes, learn these
frameworks, practice them,

00:14:43.634 --> 00:14:45.967
but towards we are enough

00:14:45.967 --> 00:14:49.769
normally to learn 50 frameworks
subdivided some parts.

00:14:49.769 --> 00:14:52.163
And then the last thought
regarding the case interview.

00:14:52.163 --> 00:14:53.568
What really helped me

00:14:53.568 --> 00:14:55.738
when I prepare for my interviews

00:14:55.738 --> 00:14:58.384
is that I paired, I put together with,

00:14:58.384 --> 00:15:00.265
I think it was a
wonderful lady at the time

00:15:00.265 --> 00:15:02.052
that interviewed also with BCG

00:15:02.052 --> 00:15:03.428
and some other firms.

00:15:03.428 --> 00:15:06.393
And we even met probably
once a week or so.

00:15:06.393 --> 00:15:07.226
And we sat together

00:15:07.226 --> 00:15:10.611
and for each session we
prepared a case for each other.

00:15:10.611 --> 00:15:13.479
And then we always
interviewed the other person.

00:15:13.479 --> 00:15:15.332
And this really worked out pretty well

00:15:15.332 --> 00:15:16.722
because this day we both got practice.

00:15:16.722 --> 00:15:19.366
We also got a bit of an experience
of being an interviewer,

00:15:19.366 --> 00:15:21.381
because this is also an
interesting experience.

00:15:21.381 --> 00:15:23.424
What do you look out for
them for the other candidate?

00:15:23.424 --> 00:15:24.600
And then we gave us feedback.

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:26.563
So I would very much encourage you

00:15:26.563 --> 00:15:28.394
because I believe the case
interviews are practice,

00:15:28.394 --> 00:15:30.788
so the more you practice it the better.

00:15:30.788 --> 00:15:32.194
And you should not only
practice by reading,

00:15:32.194 --> 00:15:33.071
but you should really practice

00:15:33.071 --> 00:15:35.950
by doing some actual more interviews.

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:37.009
This would be helpful to you.

00:15:37.009 --> 00:15:38.300
This is what really will help you

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:40.587
then get in the actual interview.

00:15:40.587 --> 00:15:43.629
And then finally the experience interview,

00:15:43.629 --> 00:15:45.944
or some people also call
it the impact interview.

00:15:45.944 --> 00:15:48.643
This is an interview type
where the interviewer

00:15:48.643 --> 00:15:51.541
will ask you to talk
about specific situations,

00:15:51.541 --> 00:15:54.762
specific instances that
you faced in your life,

00:15:54.762 --> 00:15:57.470
what happened there and
how you reacted to that.

00:15:57.470 --> 00:16:00.136
And here, common
questions are things like,

00:16:00.136 --> 00:16:02.742
okay, please talk about a situation

00:16:02.742 --> 00:16:05.030
where you had an interpersonal conflict

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:06.282
with somebody in your team

00:16:06.282 --> 00:16:07.956
and how you solved it.

00:16:07.956 --> 00:16:09.288
Or maybe you had a conflict

00:16:09.288 --> 00:16:10.655
on a content matter, right?

00:16:10.655 --> 00:16:12.250
So you disagreed with another person

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:15.741
what the best way is to move forward here,

00:16:15.741 --> 00:16:17.177
and then how you solved it.

00:16:17.177 --> 00:16:20.128
Or maybe a situation where
you really showed leadership,

00:16:20.128 --> 00:16:22.995
where you really had an impact
that you're really proud of.

00:16:22.995 --> 00:16:23.828
And what did you do?

00:16:23.828 --> 00:16:24.868
How did you achieve that?

00:16:24.868 --> 00:16:26.387
So questions like that.

00:16:26.387 --> 00:16:29.209
And then where you are
supposed to come up with

00:16:29.209 --> 00:16:31.256
tangible situations to talk about that,

00:16:31.256 --> 00:16:33.743
where you really highlight
your leadership abilities,

00:16:33.743 --> 00:16:35.544
your leadership experiences,

00:16:35.544 --> 00:16:38.235
and how that might be
applicable in the future.

00:16:38.235 --> 00:16:41.307
And here are my three main
recommendations would be first.

00:16:41.307 --> 00:16:43.692
These questions, they're pretty easy.

00:16:43.692 --> 00:16:46.380
Its usually not huge surprises,

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:48.688
so it's pretty easy for you to foresee

00:16:48.688 --> 00:16:50.246
what types of questions will be asked,

00:16:50.246 --> 00:16:52.116
then the executive questions
that I just told you

00:16:52.116 --> 00:16:55.176
and some basic variations
of these kinds of things.

00:16:55.176 --> 00:16:56.365
So you need to prepare yourself

00:16:56.365 --> 00:16:59.387
in the way that when you
get asked these questions,

00:16:59.387 --> 00:17:00.820
you should never put
yourself in a position

00:17:00.820 --> 00:17:02.188
that then in the interview

00:17:02.188 --> 00:17:03.241
on the spot,

00:17:03.241 --> 00:17:05.381
you need to come up with a crazy story

00:17:05.381 --> 00:17:07.763
that maybe happened to a
few years ago, whatever,

00:17:07.763 --> 00:17:09.646
because this is not how it should go.

00:17:09.646 --> 00:17:10.479
And set your shots at home.

00:17:10.479 --> 00:17:13.443
Already think about situations like that.

00:17:13.443 --> 00:17:15.618
You should have them very well prepared,

00:17:15.618 --> 00:17:18.023
and you should already have an idea

00:17:18.023 --> 00:17:20.805
for these kinds of main
oaks types of questions,

00:17:20.805 --> 00:17:23.183
what situations you would talk about

00:17:23.183 --> 00:17:24.360
so that you pretty much already know

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:25.193
what you're going to say.

00:17:25.193 --> 00:17:26.026
And this is not something

00:17:26.026 --> 00:17:27.462
that you should then
come up with on the spot

00:17:27.462 --> 00:17:29.036
in these interviews.

00:17:29.036 --> 00:17:30.025
And also just to warn you here,

00:17:30.025 --> 00:17:30.995
this experience interview,

00:17:30.995 --> 00:17:33.389
especially compared
with the case interview,

00:17:33.389 --> 00:17:34.574
that's something that I feel like

00:17:34.574 --> 00:17:37.188
many people significantly underestimates.

00:17:37.188 --> 00:17:38.709
They've put lots of time and practice

00:17:38.709 --> 00:17:39.762
on the case interview,

00:17:39.762 --> 00:17:41.063
but they don't really spend a lot of time

00:17:41.063 --> 00:17:43.032
at all on this experience interview part,

00:17:43.032 --> 00:17:46.967
but at least what I know about
this experience interview

00:17:46.967 --> 00:17:49.487
is often, at least as important
as the case interview.

00:17:49.487 --> 00:17:50.830
So really do your favorite favor

00:17:50.830 --> 00:17:53.728
to also get the preparation
down for that as well,

00:17:53.728 --> 00:17:57.978
and just make sure that
you have all of this ready.

00:17:58.922 --> 00:18:00.130
So this is just my little input.

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:00.963
Mayam, of course,

00:18:00.963 --> 00:18:03.211
very happy to get your question.

00:18:03.211 --> 00:18:04.569
- Well, first of all, Heinrich,

00:18:04.569 --> 00:18:06.934
thanks so much for such
an insightful session

00:18:06.934 --> 00:18:09.148
and no worries that you
went a little bit overtime,

00:18:09.148 --> 00:18:10.669
actually that gave me some time to breathe

00:18:10.669 --> 00:18:11.624
and now I'm back.

00:18:11.624 --> 00:18:13.887
(laughs)

00:18:13.887 --> 00:18:14.861
- All right.

00:18:14.861 --> 00:18:17.111
- So, all the participants,

00:18:18.066 --> 00:18:19.965
many of you submitted questions

00:18:19.965 --> 00:18:21.082
during the registration process

00:18:21.082 --> 00:18:22.496
and we have it right now.

00:18:22.496 --> 00:18:24.270
So I will start with those questions.

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:27.993
Meanwhile, feel free to ask
questions live right now.

00:18:27.993 --> 00:18:30.865
You can raise your hand
and then I will unmute you

00:18:30.865 --> 00:18:35.865
and you can phrase your
question directly to Heinrich.

00:18:35.908 --> 00:18:38.138
So to start with Heinrich,

00:18:38.138 --> 00:18:39.540
you have a pretty interesting background

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:42.790
with degrees from several universities,

00:18:43.768 --> 00:18:45.278
and in different fields.

00:18:45.278 --> 00:18:48.261
So how was that overall
experience of education for you?

00:18:48.261 --> 00:18:50.959
And how did it help you and shape you

00:18:50.959 --> 00:18:53.042
with your career journey?

00:18:54.015 --> 00:18:54.848
- Yap.

00:18:54.848 --> 00:18:55.681
And so to be honest,

00:18:55.681 --> 00:18:57.339
also talking about that a couple of times,

00:18:57.339 --> 00:19:00.186
for me, probably the most formative time

00:19:00.186 --> 00:19:01.789
was while I studied in Munich,

00:19:01.789 --> 00:19:03.905
at the University of Munich at LMU.

00:19:03.905 --> 00:19:05.462
I think this is really the time where,

00:19:05.462 --> 00:19:07.856
from an academic
perspective I learned most.

00:19:07.856 --> 00:19:08.807
And even though the later,

00:19:08.807 --> 00:19:10.536
I was in London at LSE,

00:19:10.536 --> 00:19:12.978
which is also considered
a good private university.

00:19:12.978 --> 00:19:14.972
Later, also I did an MBA,

00:19:14.972 --> 00:19:15.805
also of course,

00:19:15.805 --> 00:19:16.933
very expensive program

00:19:16.933 --> 00:19:19.343
where you can spend lots
and lots of money on that.

00:19:19.343 --> 00:19:21.243
For me, really the most
formative experiences

00:19:21.243 --> 00:19:23.980
is what I've done here in Munich at LMU.

00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:25.187
And you guys have TUM.

00:19:25.187 --> 00:19:26.379
So just kinda encourage you

00:19:26.379 --> 00:19:27.975
just to take advantage of that experience

00:19:27.975 --> 00:19:29.360
as much as possible

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:31.380
to engage in all the different,

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:33.157
student clubs, talk to other people,

00:19:33.157 --> 00:19:35.892
there's also great and vibrant
startup scene in Munich.

00:19:35.892 --> 00:19:38.829
This is really where I
just took away a lot from.

00:19:38.829 --> 00:19:42.995
And on my specific
background after high school,

00:19:42.995 --> 00:19:44.517
I just wanted to study business,

00:19:44.517 --> 00:19:47.520
but I was always also
interested in philosophy.

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.086
But for me, it was also
a bit of an experiment

00:19:49.086 --> 00:19:51.103
just to try to do a double degree.

