consumer goods,

00:30:34.455 --> 00:30:35.910
also the digital thing.

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:38.610
So companies like Google,
Facebook and so on.

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:40.700
I mean, it doesn't want to say

00:30:40.700 --> 00:30:41.908
that there are no opportunities

00:30:41.908 --> 00:30:42.886
at all with these companies,

00:30:42.886 --> 00:30:45.677
but they're not better fiend to consulting

00:30:45.677 --> 00:30:50.427
than these more old-school
German tech companies I'd say.

00:30:51.462 --> 00:30:53.629
- We have another question

00:30:54.467 --> 00:30:57.374
that goes in line with
this one in our Q&amp;A.

00:30:57.374 --> 00:31:01.147
So the participant asks
that would you recommend

00:31:01.147 --> 00:31:05.230
to get into in-house
consulting of the industries

00:31:06.125 --> 00:31:07.948
that you have mentioned?

00:31:07.948 --> 00:31:09.860
And is it possible to
have a transition from

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:13.565
in-house consulting in this
industries to the Big Four

00:31:13.565 --> 00:31:16.097
or big three, let's say?

00:31:16.097 --> 00:31:16.930
- Yeah.

00:31:17.886 --> 00:31:19.679
So it depends on your goal I'd say.

00:31:19.679 --> 00:31:22.097
So I think the main reason why
people do in all consulting

00:31:22.097 --> 00:31:25.068
is because they believe
it's pretty much comparable

00:31:25.068 --> 00:31:27.101
to a trainee program I'd say.

00:31:27.101 --> 00:31:29.334
So you started in-house consulting firm,

00:31:29.334 --> 00:31:31.233
and then you have these
different projects,

00:31:31.233 --> 00:31:34.328
and for that, you have exposure
to different departments,

00:31:34.328 --> 00:31:36.265
you get to know different people,

00:31:36.265 --> 00:31:39.492
and then you build a
network in that company

00:31:39.492 --> 00:31:42.096
and this will enable you to
hopefully (mumbles) the idea

00:31:42.096 --> 00:31:45.233
to then do a nice transition
to a good senior role.

00:31:45.233 --> 00:31:47.808
And I believe this is a valid rationale,

00:31:47.808 --> 00:31:48.684
it kind of work.

00:31:48.684 --> 00:31:52.198
And of course, then the
reputation of the investment firms

00:31:52.198 --> 00:31:53.633
differ, but from a firms,

00:31:53.633 --> 00:31:56.932
so obviously Zeeman's
has a very renowned thing

00:31:56.932 --> 00:31:59.166
with Zeeman's management consultants.

00:31:59.166 --> 00:32:03.147
And some other programs are
probably not that established,

00:32:03.147 --> 00:32:04.279
but if this is your goal,

00:32:04.279 --> 00:32:06.424
then this is, I'd say viable.

00:32:06.424 --> 00:32:08.532
I mean, I didn't do that first-hand,

00:32:08.532 --> 00:32:10.635
but it is from what I've
seen also in some companies,

00:32:10.635 --> 00:32:12.085
I've worked together with these

00:32:12.085 --> 00:32:13.850
and I was consulting people.

00:32:13.850 --> 00:32:15.238
What I believe is not a good idea

00:32:15.238 --> 00:32:18.338
is to start in an
in-house consulting firm,

00:32:18.338 --> 00:32:20.990
just to then, later to
do the switch to MBB

00:32:20.990 --> 00:32:22.240
or to Big Four.

00:32:23.324 --> 00:32:25.241
At least from McKinsey,

00:32:26.809 --> 00:32:27.642
I think about,

00:32:27.642 --> 00:32:29.308
I cannot imagine a senior guy

00:32:29.308 --> 00:32:32.020
who transitioned from
in-house consulting firm.

00:32:32.020 --> 00:32:34.179
This is not to say that it's impossible,

00:32:34.179 --> 00:32:36.275
but also just in terms of the story,

00:32:36.275 --> 00:32:38.394
I just feel like it
doesn't make much sense.

00:32:38.394 --> 00:32:39.649
I mean, if you want to do consulting,

00:32:39.649 --> 00:32:41.302
then why wouldn't you directly start

00:32:41.302 --> 00:32:43.129
with the big consulting firms.

00:32:43.129 --> 00:32:44.407
And if you want to corporate

00:32:44.407 --> 00:32:46.137
then rather start in a corporate role.

00:32:46.137 --> 00:32:48.798
But I mean, I think you need
to be very (audio glitch)

00:32:48.798 --> 00:32:50.298
you can tell a convincing story here,

00:32:50.298 --> 00:32:52.794
like what your rationale
should be about (mumbles).

00:32:52.794 --> 00:32:53.816
Probably not the best idea.

00:32:53.816 --> 00:32:54.924
It's not impossible,

00:32:54.924 --> 00:32:57.630
but that wouldn't necessarily recommend.

00:32:57.630 --> 00:32:58.463
- Great.

00:33:00.201 --> 00:33:02.247
Alberto, you are next.

00:33:02.247 --> 00:33:04.023
- [Alberto] Hello, can you hear me?

00:33:04.023 --> 00:33:05.458
- Yes.

00:33:05.458 --> 00:33:07.139
- [Alberto] Hi, everyone.

00:33:07.139 --> 00:33:09.498
And thank you for the sessions.

00:33:09.498 --> 00:33:12.215
It's a really helpful and informative,

00:33:12.215 --> 00:33:14.747
so really appreciate it.

00:33:14.747 --> 00:33:16.680
My question is,

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:20.278
so, I'm currently in my
second-last semester at TUM,

00:33:20.278 --> 00:33:22.450
for my master's degree in management.

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:27.079
And I was thinking about
applying to the Big Three

00:33:27.079 --> 00:33:29.948
in their Italian offices,

00:33:29.948 --> 00:33:32.371
'cause that's where I'm from.

00:33:32.371 --> 00:33:34.804
Therefore I wanted to ask you,

00:33:34.804 --> 00:33:36.422
if to your knowledge,

00:33:36.422 --> 00:33:38.769
if there are any variations

00:33:38.769 --> 00:33:42.017
or differences in the recruiting process,

00:33:42.017 --> 00:33:45.434
depending on where the office is located.

00:33:46.318 --> 00:33:48.076
And where could you find information

00:33:48.076 --> 00:33:50.511
on how to account for
that and prepare for that.

00:33:50.511 --> 00:33:53.011
And also how much do McKinsey,

00:33:54.757 --> 00:33:58.239
BCG and Bain value
international experiences.

00:33:58.239 --> 00:33:59.989
And if that's actually

00:33:59.989 --> 00:34:01.399
something that can make a difference,

00:34:01.399 --> 00:34:04.752
or you should just focus on other things.

00:34:04.752 --> 00:34:05.585
So yeah.

00:34:05.585 --> 00:34:06.418
Thank you.

00:34:06.418 --> 00:34:07.692
- Yeah, of course.

00:34:07.692 --> 00:34:09.577
So first, I mean, yes,

00:34:09.577 --> 00:34:10.721
there can be some differences

00:34:10.721 --> 00:34:13.415
regarding the application process.

00:34:13.415 --> 00:34:15.234
Usually, at least with the larger firms

00:34:15.234 --> 00:34:17.999
these differences are
not huge, but they exist.

00:34:17.999 --> 00:34:19.367
Just to give you an example,

00:34:19.367 --> 00:34:21.714
when I started at McKinsey

00:34:21.714 --> 00:34:23.843
there still was something that was called

00:34:23.843 --> 00:34:26.823
the PSP problem solving tests.

00:34:26.823 --> 00:34:28.748
It was pretty much,

00:34:28.748 --> 00:34:29.731
I think somebody has,

00:34:29.731 --> 00:34:31.679
maybe somebody can put himself on mute

00:34:31.679 --> 00:34:35.260
because I'm hearing myself double.

00:34:35.260 --> 00:34:36.427
Some red echo.

00:34:37.330 --> 00:34:39.244
So there was something like the PST,

00:34:39.244 --> 00:34:40.469
the problem solving tests,

00:34:40.469 --> 00:34:41.760
and this was kind of a written test

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:43.892
where you needed to answer

00:34:43.892 --> 00:34:46.359
little-like math problems.

00:34:46.359 --> 00:34:48.287
So you were really sitting
there in the office

00:34:48.287 --> 00:34:49.560
when you conducted the interviews,

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:51.667
and then they had like
a little written test.

00:34:51.667 --> 00:34:52.922
And I think in Germany at McKinsey's,

00:34:52.922 --> 00:34:54.646
now they don't have it anymore,

00:34:54.646 --> 00:34:58.257
but in some other countries
I think they still do.

00:34:58.257 --> 00:35:01.053
And then another thing that
is now currently tested

00:35:01.053 --> 00:35:02.548
at McKinsey, as far as I know,

00:35:02.548 --> 00:35:06.499
it's kind of an online game that they use.

00:35:06.499 --> 00:35:07.545
I believe they use it

00:35:07.545 --> 00:35:09.210
also in the very first twinning process.

00:35:09.210 --> 00:35:12.226
So this is something that
candidates needs to do at home.

00:35:12.226 --> 00:35:13.642
And it's kind of a little game,

00:35:13.642 --> 00:35:15.619
it's about equal systems

00:35:15.619 --> 00:35:18.015
where they need to make
sure that all the ecosystems

00:35:18.015 --> 00:35:19.165
are imbalanced or something.

00:35:19.165 --> 00:35:20.040
There's other thing you can also

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:22.468
on the McKinsey career page,

00:35:22.468 --> 00:35:24.467
you can find some basic
information on that.

00:35:24.467 --> 00:35:27.535
But I believe this is not
there in every country yet,

00:35:27.535 --> 00:35:28.932
because they're just slowly testing it

00:35:28.932 --> 00:35:30.385
and then rolling it out to one of them.

00:35:30.385 --> 00:35:31.218
I'm not a hundred percent sure

00:35:31.218 --> 00:35:34.332
whether the moment it's
already live in Germany or not.

00:35:34.332 --> 00:35:35.549
I mean, to find out,

00:35:35.549 --> 00:35:38.561
you should just check the
career page of the local office.

00:35:38.561 --> 00:35:39.992
So if you go to a McKinsey,

00:35:39.992 --> 00:35:43.042
like the German McKinsey
page and they are on kiryl,

00:35:43.042 --> 00:35:44.134
you will find the basic information

00:35:44.134 --> 00:35:45.731
on how it's run in Germany.

00:35:45.731 --> 00:35:47.934
And I trust the same thing will be true

00:35:47.934 --> 00:35:49.997
if you then go on the Italian page.

00:35:49.997 --> 00:35:51.363
So check out your local recruiting page,

00:35:51.363 --> 00:35:53.814
and then there will be
the key information,

00:35:53.814 --> 00:35:54.974
and then at least after you apply it,

00:35:54.974 --> 00:35:56.672
and then if you get
invited to an interview

00:35:56.672 --> 00:35:58.532
then usually you will also get

00:35:58.532 --> 00:36:00.837
lots of additional
information from the HR team.

00:36:00.837 --> 00:36:02.308
The whole executive
interviews will be random

00:36:02.308 --> 00:36:03.916
and what would be expected from you.

00:36:03.916 --> 00:36:04.849
So of course,

00:36:04.849 --> 00:36:07.245
we get that information
then and beforehand.

00:36:07.245 --> 00:36:11.114
And regarding the
international experience.

00:36:11.114 --> 00:36:14.079
So I think what's usually
not that valued is,

00:36:14.079 --> 00:36:15.327
frankly is languages.

00:36:15.327 --> 00:36:16.287
So not having language skills

00:36:16.287 --> 00:36:19.410
and lots and lots of different languages

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:22.750
is usually not considered
super important or whatever.

00:36:22.750 --> 00:36:25.011
I think what's of course
important is English.

00:36:25.011 --> 00:36:27.406
So that you have to go
through an English case.

00:36:27.406 --> 00:36:28.239
And of course,

00:36:28.239 --> 00:36:31.186
what many consulting firms
want is language skills

00:36:31.186 --> 00:36:32.393
in the local office.

00:36:32.393 --> 00:36:35.526
So, if you want to apply for German role,

00:36:35.526 --> 00:36:38.129
especially for client facing role,

00:36:38.129 --> 00:36:38.962
then yes,

00:36:38.962 --> 00:36:41.752
people will expect from you
not necessarily to be fluent,

00:36:41.752 --> 00:36:45.187
but at least to have kind of
business level German skills.

00:36:45.187 --> 00:36:46.659
And the same as to also
for other countries

00:36:46.659 --> 00:36:48.191
for their respective language.

00:36:48.191 --> 00:36:49.935
But usually it's not a huge factor

00:36:49.935 --> 00:36:53.078
on whether you can speak
other languages as well.

00:36:53.078 --> 00:36:54.583
Of course, though it doesn't hurt,

00:36:54.583 --> 00:36:56.767
and it will enable you
potentially also to do projects

00:36:56.767 --> 00:36:58.130
in that country,

00:36:58.130 --> 00:37:01.815
if you want to go there
then during your engagement.

00:37:01.815 --> 00:37:04.728
Regarding, other experience
such as being abroad

00:37:04.728 --> 00:37:05.575
or something like that,

00:37:05.575 --> 00:37:08.271
having spent a semester
abroad, internship abroad,

00:37:08.271 --> 00:37:09.619
I think, yes, this is valued.

00:37:09.619 --> 00:37:13.351
So again, it's more like a hygiene factor,

00:37:13.351 --> 00:37:14.346
in the sense that I believe

00:37:14.346 --> 00:37:16.792
if people kind of expect you to have it.

00:37:16.792 --> 00:37:18.389
So I believe if you have it,

00:37:18.389 --> 00:37:19.598
it's not like a huge PLASP.

00:37:19.598 --> 00:37:20.581
People will not be like,

00:37:20.581 --> 00:37:23.201
wow, this guy's so great
because he was abroad,

00:37:23.201 --> 00:37:24.617
but it's more like the other way round.

00:37:24.617 --> 00:37:25.996
If you don't have it,

00:37:25.996 --> 00:37:29.819
then people will rather
notice it in a negative way.

00:37:29.819 --> 00:37:32.647
And this is not to say
that your chances are zero.

00:37:32.647 --> 00:37:33.480
If you have never worked abroad,

00:37:33.480 --> 00:37:36.285
a few have never done a semester abroad,

00:37:36.285 --> 00:37:37.535
so it's still possible to get in,

00:37:37.535 --> 00:37:40.603
but this will be seen
as something negative.

00:37:40.603 --> 00:37:42.591
And the rationale, here again is just,

00:37:42.591 --> 00:37:45.331
you live up frequently with
people from other countries.

00:37:45.331 --> 00:37:46.899
Also, me working at McKinsey,

00:37:46.899 --> 00:37:49.353
probably 50% of my studies were abroad,

00:37:49.353 --> 00:37:50.682
but not in Germany.

00:37:50.682 --> 00:37:53.896
So you will also be staff
potentially on studies

00:37:53.896 --> 00:37:54.975
outside of Germany

00:37:54.975 --> 00:37:56.102
or outside of the country.

00:37:56.102 --> 00:37:58.914
And there, you can just give
the people a bit of confidence

00:37:58.914 --> 00:38:00.659
that you will handle the situation well

00:38:00.659 --> 00:38:05.439
if you already show some
international experience.

00:38:05.439 --> 00:38:06.805
- Thank you, Heinrich.

00:38:06.805 --> 00:38:08.344
- Sure.

00:38:08.344 --> 00:38:09.684
I just realized we are approaching

00:38:09.684 --> 00:38:12.279
towards the end of the session

00:38:12.279 --> 00:38:15.120
and there are still a
lot of hands that are--

00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:17.049
- We can go a bit over time if you want.

00:38:17.049 --> 00:38:18.411
I mean, I'm flexible.

00:38:18.411 --> 00:38:19.336
- That's great.

00:38:19.336 --> 00:38:23.686
And I would try to integrate
as many as questions possible.

00:38:23.686 --> 00:38:24.874
- Mm-hmm.

00:38:24.874 --> 00:38:26.624
- So next question is

00:38:27.932 --> 00:38:31.205
are salaries is higher
in consulting invest

00:38:31.205 --> 00:38:34.372
in South Germany than in East Germany.

00:38:36.013 --> 00:38:37.438
- So, I mean, yeah

00:38:37.438 --> 00:38:39.553
there might be differences
not far but (mumbles)

00:38:39.553 --> 00:38:40.956
but my general take on that is,

00:38:40.956 --> 00:38:43.330
or maybe just to start even
on the international level.

00:38:43.330 --> 00:38:45.753
So even within one company at BCG

00:38:45.753 --> 00:38:47.614
or McKinsey or whatever, yes,

00:38:47.614 --> 00:38:50.584
salaries will be different
country-by-country.

00:38:50.584 --> 00:38:54.262
So it will be adopted to the
local competitive situation

00:38:54.262 --> 00:38:56.856
to the local living costs and so on.

00:38:56.856 --> 00:38:59.735
And actually Germany
has pretty good salaries

00:38:59.735 --> 00:39:00.568
actually I'd say,

00:39:00.568 --> 00:39:02.374
compared to other countries
in consulting in general.

00:39:02.374 --> 00:39:04.654
This is also true for banking.

00:39:04.654 --> 00:39:06.904
So if you go to U.K. or something,

00:39:06.904 --> 00:39:09.116
I mean, especially then
if you're in London,

00:39:09.116 --> 00:39:10.376
especially given that
the other living costs

00:39:10.376 --> 00:39:11.679
are so much higher,

00:39:11.679 --> 00:39:14.339
I believe the salaries in
Germany are really attractive

00:39:14.339 --> 00:39:16.012
and very interesting.

00:39:16.012 --> 00:39:16.939
Now within Germany,

00:39:16.939 --> 00:39:20.856
at least with the big
firms like BCG, McKinsey,

00:39:21.838 --> 00:39:23.017
also the Big Four,

00:39:23.017 --> 00:39:24.495
you should usually expect

00:39:24.495 --> 00:39:28.458
that they will not
differentiate with in Germany.

00:39:28.458 --> 00:39:29.291
And the reason also is

00:39:29.291 --> 00:39:30.262
that at least at McKinsey,

00:39:30.262 --> 00:39:33.606
you can pretty much freely
change offices between Germany.

00:39:33.606 --> 00:39:36.683
So I started actually
in the Berlin office,

00:39:36.683 --> 00:39:39.751
and then I transitioned to
the Munich office later,

00:39:39.751 --> 00:39:41.055
just due to private reasons

00:39:41.055 --> 00:39:43.989
because my wife moved there.

00:39:43.989 --> 00:39:45.360
So for these reasons

00:39:45.360 --> 00:39:46.193
in general, no,

00:39:46.193 --> 00:39:47.824
they will not differentiate
between Eastern

00:39:47.824 --> 00:39:50.062
and Western Germany,

00:39:50.062 --> 00:39:50.895
though of course,

00:39:50.895 --> 00:39:52.761
now if you're looking into
smaller consulting firms,

00:39:52.761 --> 00:39:55.805
smaller boutiques or whatever,

00:39:55.805 --> 00:39:57.205
then maybe,

00:39:57.205 --> 00:39:58.444
I mean, in general, of course,

00:39:58.444 --> 00:40:02.027
it's true that in general
the average salaries

00:40:02.027 --> 00:40:03.963
in the East are lower than in the West.

00:40:03.963 --> 00:40:04.796
- I see.

00:40:06.351 --> 00:40:07.846
I'll take another participant question

00:40:07.846 --> 00:40:09.809
from the Q&amp;A section.

00:40:09.809 --> 00:40:11.856
And the question is,

00:40:11.856 --> 00:40:14.279
it's no secret that you
need really good grades

00:40:14.279 --> 00:40:15.715
to get into consulting.

00:40:15.715 --> 00:40:17.083
Do you think it's problematic

00:40:17.083 --> 00:40:19.906
or even a no-go if you
didn't finish your degree

00:40:19.906 --> 00:40:22.518
within the regular time period,

00:40:22.518 --> 00:40:26.591
and you took one or two semester longer?

00:40:26.591 --> 00:40:28.301
- No, I don't think so.

00:40:28.301 --> 00:40:29.142
I mean, of course,

00:40:29.142 --> 00:40:32.860
it's good to then have
some kind of rationale,

00:40:32.860 --> 00:40:36.072
but I mean, it's always possible
to come up with some more

00:40:36.072 --> 00:40:37.271
or less convincing reason

00:40:37.271 --> 00:40:38.583
why you did that.

00:40:38.583 --> 00:40:40.064
I mean, I'm sure you have a reason,

00:40:40.064 --> 00:40:41.773
otherwise you would have done it faster,

00:40:41.773 --> 00:40:42.830
so maybe you've been abroad

00:40:42.830 --> 00:40:44.860
or maybe you did some cool internships,

00:40:44.860 --> 00:40:46.847
maybe you did something
else from paradigms

00:40:46.847 --> 00:40:48.365
that really took your time.

00:40:48.365 --> 00:40:51.370
And then I do not think
that this is a huge issue.

00:40:51.370 --> 00:40:53.647
Of course, then if it took longer

00:40:53.647 --> 00:40:55.908
and then also your
grades are not that good

00:40:55.908 --> 00:40:56.741
and so on.

00:40:56.741 --> 00:40:58.766
I mean, then this can add
up to maybe an impression,

00:40:58.766 --> 00:41:00.251
which is not that great.

00:41:00.251 --> 00:41:02.347
Of course, it's helpful
to have competed it

00:41:02.347 --> 00:41:04.014
in the regular time.

00:41:04.873 --> 00:41:08.528
But this in most cases should
not be a huge tiebreaker

00:41:08.528 --> 00:41:11.137
- Okay, so there's a question
from Jan in the chatbox,

00:41:11.137 --> 00:41:13.406
and also a couple of participants

00:41:13.406 --> 00:41:15.889
during registration
submitted the same question,

00:41:15.889 --> 00:41:18.705
that they have a couple
of years of experience

00:41:18.705 --> 00:41:21.639
raging from two to five years.

00:41:21.639 --> 00:41:25.108
And they have been working
on this for quite some time,

00:41:25.108 --> 00:41:26.579
like getting an internship

00:41:26.579 --> 00:41:31.483
into in-house consulting
or a Big Four consulting.

00:41:31.483 --> 00:41:35.512
And all the tips that you
provided in the session,

00:41:35.512 --> 00:41:36.920
in the beginning

00:41:36.920 --> 00:41:38.040
they have been working on it,

00:41:38.040 --> 00:41:40.269
on CV and cover letter
and all those stuffs,

00:41:40.269 --> 00:41:43.273
and still things are not really working.

00:41:43.273 --> 00:41:46.945
So, what would you advise to such people?

00:41:46.945 --> 00:41:50.208
- Okay, I mean, you
would really need to look

00:41:50.208 --> 00:41:51.041
into the specifics.

00:41:51.041 --> 00:41:51.874
So, I mean,

00:41:51.874 --> 00:41:53.503
it might be the case
that there's a problem

00:41:53.503 --> 00:41:55.610
with your documents.

00:41:55.610 --> 00:41:58.539
So here you should really
make sure that these are okay.

00:41:58.539 --> 00:42:01.421
So, from a former
perspective it's all there.

00:42:01.421 --> 00:42:04.017
And so I would recommend
that you really work

00:42:04.017 --> 00:42:04.978
with some people whom you trust,

00:42:04.978 --> 00:42:06.847
would be able to (mumbles) of that

00:42:06.847 --> 00:42:09.023
to then get you some feedback.

00:42:09.023 --> 00:42:10.693
And actually, if you're
interested in that,

00:42:10.693 --> 00:42:11.837
on my YouTube channel,

00:42:11.837 --> 00:42:14.429
I have recently made a video about CVs,

00:42:14.429 --> 00:42:17.213
where I actually reveal
real examples of CVs

00:42:17.213 --> 00:42:18.699
of other people.

00:42:18.699 --> 00:42:19.831
And there are also lots of tips

00:42:19.831 --> 00:42:21.276
on how to put specific things.

00:42:21.276 --> 00:42:22.109
So if are just interested in that

00:42:22.109 --> 00:42:26.265
check out this video
on my YouTube channel.

00:42:26.265 --> 00:42:28.304
But just make sure that
your material is there.

00:42:28.304 --> 00:42:31.851
And then if you still get not invited,

00:42:31.851 --> 00:42:33.884
then unfortunately,

00:42:33.884 --> 00:42:36.910
the profile just doesn't seem to meet

00:42:36.910 --> 00:42:38.458
the criteria, unfortunately.

00:42:38.458 --> 00:42:40.226
And here of course, then
I would also recommend you

00:42:40.226 --> 00:42:42.517
with probably the only shot that you have.

00:42:42.517 --> 00:42:43.967
There's just a try to network, right?

00:42:43.967 --> 00:42:45.937
So try to go maybe to career first,

00:42:45.937 --> 00:42:47.782
to some HR people,

00:42:47.782 --> 00:42:49.173
maybe then also if you
have the opportunity

00:42:49.173 --> 00:42:51.517
to talk to one person face-to-face,

00:42:51.517 --> 00:42:53.061
and maybe you can just find another guy

00:42:53.061 --> 00:42:54.688
who works at the firm

00:42:54.688 --> 00:42:56.764
who does really can get
you some extra feedback on

00:42:56.764 --> 00:42:57.597
your profile.

00:42:57.597 --> 00:43:00.930
Maybe also how you frame certain things.

00:43:02.007 --> 00:43:04.170
Then maybe you get a shot often,

00:43:04.170 --> 00:43:06.209
or at least getting
invited to the interview,

00:43:06.209 --> 00:43:08.457
because I think this was
also one of the questions.

00:43:08.457 --> 00:43:10.380
Just to quickly touch on that.

00:43:10.380 --> 00:43:11.355
The question is,

00:43:11.355 --> 00:43:12.696
does networking help?

00:43:12.696 --> 00:43:13.770
Can you get into the firm?

00:43:13.770 --> 00:43:15.106
Just because you have,

00:43:15.106 --> 00:43:16.984
Germany, you say vitamin B,

00:43:16.984 --> 00:43:20.720
so you have relationships
with food to some people.

00:43:20.720 --> 00:43:23.087
And usually for most companies

00:43:23.087 --> 00:43:25.047
you can get to the interview by that.

00:43:25.047 --> 00:43:26.094
So if you now the right people,

00:43:26.094 --> 00:43:27.321
and if they're really like you,

00:43:27.321 --> 00:43:28.593
and if you find a partner

00:43:28.593 --> 00:43:30.542
who really believes you are a great guy,

00:43:30.542 --> 00:43:32.998
he can push you to get an interview.

00:43:32.998 --> 00:43:34.068
But then from the interview,

00:43:34.068 --> 00:43:35.294
you are on yourself.

00:43:35.294 --> 00:43:38.836
I mean, in the interview you
will then be assessed by a team

00:43:38.836 --> 00:43:41.084
and then nobody will sabotage

00:43:41.084 --> 00:43:42.747
the whole recruiting process for you,

00:43:42.747 --> 00:43:43.915
just to get you through.

00:43:43.915 --> 00:43:44.958
And even if a partner would do that,

00:43:44.958 --> 00:43:46.621
that would be, probably wouldn't work.

00:43:46.621 --> 00:43:48.671
And we also support that first reputation.

00:43:48.671 --> 00:43:49.654
So yes, it can help you.

00:43:49.654 --> 00:43:50.747
Networking can help you
to get an interview,

00:43:50.747 --> 00:43:52.940
but then from the interview
you are on yourself.

00:43:52.940 --> 00:43:56.169
So if for whatever reason
your profile just isn't there,

00:43:56.169 --> 00:43:57.908
then really try to talk to these people,

00:43:57.908 --> 00:43:59.637
try to convince them,

00:43:59.637 --> 00:44:02.507
but then of course, it will be difficult.

00:44:02.507 --> 00:44:06.108
But yeah, but this isn't
the chance you have.

00:44:06.108 --> 00:44:06.941
- Thanks.

00:44:08.739 --> 00:44:09.838
Now Lorena asks,

00:44:09.838 --> 00:44:14.212
what was your struggle while
getting into consulting?

00:44:14.212 --> 00:44:17.091
If you could share a
couple of thoughts on that.

00:44:17.091 --> 00:44:18.740
And in line with it,

00:44:18.740 --> 00:44:21.746
I will quickly face next question as well.

00:44:21.746 --> 00:44:23.385
If you could also share your experiences

00:44:23.385 --> 00:44:26.898
of two excellent projects
that you did in consulting

00:44:26.898 --> 00:44:28.587
and two most challenging projects

00:44:28.587 --> 00:44:29.720
that you did in consulting.

00:44:29.720 --> 00:44:30.553
(laughs)

00:44:30.553 --> 00:44:34.012
Okay, so, my challenges, I mean,

00:44:34.012 --> 00:44:35.668
to be honest, I was kind of lucky

00:44:35.668 --> 00:44:36.989
because I was in a bachelor

00:44:36.989 --> 00:44:38.156
and I just wanted,

00:44:38.156 --> 00:44:39.642
I already did a couple of internships

00:44:39.642 --> 00:44:42.871
and I just wanted now to
do a consulting internship.

00:44:42.871 --> 00:44:44.723
And then I just applied
to a couple of firms

00:44:44.723 --> 00:44:47.388
and actually then the McKinsey one

00:44:47.388 --> 00:44:49.737
was one of the earlier interviews.

00:44:49.737 --> 00:44:51.052
And I think I was well prepared.

00:44:51.052 --> 00:44:53.590
I did lots of mock case studies.

00:44:53.590 --> 00:44:55.830
I did a couple of other interviews before,

00:44:55.830 --> 00:44:56.935
and then added the McKinsey one.

00:44:56.935 --> 00:44:58.539
And then I got in for the internship,

00:44:58.539 --> 00:45:00.467
and then actually also
canceled the other ones,

00:45:00.467 --> 00:45:02.463
because for whatever reason I always,

00:45:02.463 --> 00:45:03.828
McKinsey was kind of my dream from then,

00:45:03.828 --> 00:45:05.368
to work at that time.

00:45:05.368 --> 00:45:06.895
And then I just signed up there

00:45:06.895 --> 00:45:08.175
and then what's of course possible

00:45:08.175 --> 00:45:09.363
is once you do the internship,

00:45:09.363 --> 00:45:11.056
then if you perform well,

00:45:11.056 --> 00:45:14.433
then you can get an offer to
work with the firm afterwards.

00:45:14.433 --> 00:45:15.358
And then actually,

00:45:15.358 --> 00:45:16.898
because my master was only one year.

00:45:16.898 --> 00:45:18.580
So sometimes it's kind of the time limit

00:45:18.580 --> 00:45:19.439
that you need to,

00:45:19.439 --> 00:45:22.519
then decide to join after
a certain amount of time.

00:45:22.519 --> 00:45:23.755
But one year usually is fine.

00:45:23.755 --> 00:45:26.404
For then I was able to
basically start my master,

00:45:26.404 --> 00:45:28.726
already with the offer of then
joining McKinsey afterwards.

00:45:28.726 --> 00:45:31.807
It was of course, a super
comfortable position.

00:45:31.807 --> 00:45:33.437
So I guess I just was lucky.

00:45:33.437 --> 00:45:36.492
And for these reasons I
didn't have a huge struggle

00:45:36.492 --> 00:45:37.511
to be honest.

00:45:37.511 --> 00:45:39.772
But then of course, in consulting,

00:45:39.772 --> 00:45:42.169
not with the two best
and two worst projects,

00:45:42.169 --> 00:45:44.898
then you will struggle a lot on the job.

00:45:44.898 --> 00:45:46.973
Everybody would have bad projects

00:45:46.973 --> 00:45:48.774
and this can be super tiresome

00:45:48.774 --> 00:45:50.941
just to share two stories.

00:45:53.988 --> 00:45:55.155
One thing was,

00:45:57.523 --> 00:45:58.475
or just to (mumbles),

00:45:58.475 --> 00:46:00.577
just to talk about one thing here,

00:46:00.577 --> 00:46:01.410
I think in the end,

00:46:01.410 --> 00:46:02.630
what it comes down to,

00:46:02.630 --> 00:46:04.284
to whether you enjoy a project or not,

00:46:04.284 --> 00:46:05.642
it's not so much on the content.

00:46:05.642 --> 00:46:07.998
It's not so much on the topic,

00:46:07.998 --> 00:46:09.587
but even though of course, some topics,

00:46:09.587 --> 00:46:12.584
some projects can be more
interesting than other, no doubt,

00:46:12.584 --> 00:46:13.417
but what I believe,

00:46:13.417 --> 00:46:15.097
what most people feel like

00:46:15.097 --> 00:46:15.930
then also who I've talked to,

00:46:15.930 --> 00:46:17.272
some other colleagues,

00:46:17.272 --> 00:46:19.583
the defining factor is your team,

00:46:19.583 --> 00:46:20.482
and your clients.

00:46:20.482 --> 00:46:21.527
The team that you work with,

00:46:21.527 --> 00:46:22.564
probably number one,

00:46:22.564 --> 00:46:24.780
and then the clients who
work with number two.

00:46:24.780 --> 00:46:27.685
And I was once in a tough situation

00:46:27.685 --> 00:46:32.459
where I didn't get along way
with the team I work with,

00:46:32.459 --> 00:46:35.326
and also didn't got
along way with the client

00:46:35.326 --> 00:46:38.008
or with the main clients
that I was responsible for.

00:46:38.008 --> 00:46:40.999
And this really was a difficult situation,

00:46:40.999 --> 00:46:42.764
because after some time,

00:46:42.764 --> 00:46:43.597
you need to,

00:46:43.597 --> 00:46:45.602
every Monday morning you fly there,

00:46:45.602 --> 00:46:47.226
then you get into the team room,

00:46:47.226 --> 00:46:49.467
and then there's the weird
atmosphere in the team room,

00:46:49.467 --> 00:46:50.729
then you talk to the clients,

00:46:50.729 --> 00:46:53.171
then a weird atmosphere with the clients.

00:46:53.171 --> 00:46:55.167
And then, also then on top of that,

00:46:55.167 --> 00:46:57.155
the stress of working late every day

00:46:57.155 --> 00:47:00.406
of then talking to the partners
who are unhappy and so on.

00:47:00.406 --> 00:47:01.487
I mean, this is really an experience

00:47:01.487 --> 00:47:02.654
that was just,

00:47:03.512 --> 00:47:05.015
its so tiresome

00:47:05.015 --> 00:47:07.332
Somebody crushes you so much
of the time psychologically,

00:47:07.332 --> 00:47:10.301
but here I think the good
part is in consulting.

00:47:10.301 --> 00:47:11.157
And I think everybody,

00:47:11.157 --> 00:47:13.618
if you're work in consulting
at some point in time,

00:47:13.618 --> 00:47:14.830
you would have an experience like that,

00:47:14.830 --> 00:47:15.758
but everybody will have one

00:47:15.758 --> 00:47:17.416
or two bad projects you can talk about.

00:47:17.416 --> 00:47:18.296
But the good thing is,

00:47:18.296 --> 00:47:22.176
and I think this is really a
contrast compared to industry,

00:47:22.176 --> 00:47:23.938
after two, three, four, five months,

00:47:23.938 --> 00:47:25.551
once a project is over,

00:47:25.551 --> 00:47:27.271
the cards are usually mixed again,

00:47:27.271 --> 00:47:29.943
so you can work together
with new colleagues,

00:47:29.943 --> 00:47:32.350
you can work with a new company

00:47:32.350 --> 00:47:34.653
and you can basically start
again from a fresh slate.

00:47:34.653 --> 00:47:36.738
Then of course, over time,

00:47:36.738 --> 00:47:38.182
you really get to know the people

00:47:38.182 --> 00:47:39.756
you really enjoyed working with,

00:47:39.756 --> 00:47:41.531
the one partner you really enjoyed.

00:47:41.531 --> 00:47:43.212
The project leads really laughed,

00:47:43.212 --> 00:47:44.487
was really a great guy.

00:47:44.487 --> 00:47:46.337
And then you can stick
together with these people

00:47:46.337 --> 00:47:48.624
and then try to work more
and more with these people.

00:47:48.624 --> 00:47:49.712
And then this is how you create

00:47:49.712 --> 00:47:51.819
the soul kind of set of
people who support each other.

00:47:51.819 --> 00:47:54.045
And then there's just a great experience.

00:47:54.045 --> 00:47:55.212
If you contrast that with corporate,

00:47:55.212 --> 00:47:58.157
you start in a team in corporate,

00:47:58.157 --> 00:47:59.970
you have these teammates,

00:47:59.970 --> 00:48:00.918
you have this one boss,

00:48:00.918 --> 00:48:02.918
you're one team that you will work with.

00:48:02.918 --> 00:48:05.950
And then usually for the
next three, four, five years,

00:48:05.950 --> 00:48:06.946
this would be your family.

00:48:06.946 --> 00:48:07.904
And you can either be lucky,

00:48:07.904 --> 00:48:09.662
and it works out really well.

00:48:09.662 --> 00:48:10.594
Or for whatever reason,

00:48:10.594 --> 00:48:11.846
you can be really unlucky

00:48:11.846 --> 00:48:13.704
and you just really don't get along

00:48:13.704 --> 00:48:14.891
with these people at all.

00:48:14.891 --> 00:48:16.109
And then basically your only option

00:48:16.109 --> 00:48:17.705
is to switch jobs.

00:48:17.705 --> 00:48:20.195
I mean, you will never hear that.

00:48:20.195 --> 00:48:22.028
I mean, if you're really
some point in the position,

00:48:22.028 --> 00:48:24.674
then at some point you just need to leave.

00:48:24.674 --> 00:48:25.875
I mean, this is just about consulting

00:48:25.875 --> 00:48:27.609
this to lots of flexibility,

00:48:27.609 --> 00:48:29.552
just to talk about a
positive example is red.

00:48:29.552 --> 00:48:31.697
This is when you have all
these things come together.

00:48:31.697 --> 00:48:33.296
So it was

00:48:33.296 --> 00:48:35.318
just one of my recent ones,

00:48:35.318 --> 00:48:37.217
it was an automotive company,

00:48:37.217 --> 00:48:38.384
an automotive supplier.

00:48:38.384 --> 00:48:40.148
And the topic itself was interesting,

00:48:40.148 --> 00:48:41.934
but not super exciting.

00:48:41.934 --> 00:48:44.863
But we really got along with
the clients really well.

00:48:44.863 --> 00:48:46.860
There were also some nice junior people.

00:48:46.860 --> 00:48:47.693
Then on the evening,

00:48:47.693 --> 00:48:49.320
sometimes we would go out with them,

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:50.911
on the weekends we would ...

00:48:50.911 --> 00:48:51.914
Because I was living in
the same city with them

00:48:51.914 --> 00:48:52.987
we would meet,

00:48:52.987 --> 00:48:54.736
we would have some drinks.

00:48:54.736 --> 00:48:55.924
The team was super cool.

00:48:55.924 --> 00:48:57.240
We all got along super well,

00:48:57.240 --> 00:48:58.663
the product wasn't going well,

00:48:58.663 --> 00:48:59.621
everybody was helping.

00:48:59.621 --> 00:49:01.343
Everybody was just having a good time.

00:49:01.343 --> 00:49:02.462
The impact wasn't bad,

00:49:02.462 --> 00:49:03.803
or worked up really great.

00:49:03.803 --> 00:49:05.582
With this of course, also always cool.

00:49:05.582 --> 00:49:07.127
If you can see that what you're doing

00:49:07.127 --> 00:49:09.118
really impacts the
company in a positive way,

00:49:09.118 --> 00:49:10.772
and you're really helping.

00:49:10.772 --> 00:49:12.928
And if all of this comes together,

00:49:12.928 --> 00:49:13.815
then it just so great.

00:49:13.815 --> 00:49:15.988
And then you also don't mind
anymore if you work late

00:49:15.988 --> 00:49:18.292
or if you work until late in the evening

00:49:18.292 --> 00:49:19.315
or something like that.

00:49:19.315 --> 00:49:22.040
This is really when you
love doing your job,

00:49:22.040 --> 00:49:24.428
and you really love coming
into the office every day.

00:49:24.428 --> 00:49:26.338
And the truth is such, will
not be like that every day.

00:49:26.338 --> 00:49:28.933
But you will also come
across these products,

00:49:28.933 --> 00:49:30.264
and they're just amazing.

00:49:30.264 --> 00:49:31.097
And then over time

00:49:31.097 --> 00:49:32.397
you could really choose more and more,

00:49:32.397 --> 00:49:33.297
the people you wanna work with.

00:49:33.297 --> 00:49:34.130
And this is just,

00:49:34.130 --> 00:49:38.408
I believe the magic of
working in consulting.

00:49:38.408 --> 00:49:42.019
- Yeah, quite exciting, I would say.

00:49:42.019 --> 00:49:43.368
And the good and bad

00:49:43.368 --> 00:49:45.219
both things are that it will end.

00:49:45.219 --> 00:49:46.111
I mean,

00:49:46.111 --> 00:49:48.116
(laughs)

00:49:48.116 --> 00:49:48.949
- Exactly.

00:49:49.826 --> 00:49:53.015
- So I'll take the next question from Rue.

00:49:53.015 --> 00:49:56.805
He asks what is the
percentage of non-Germans

00:49:56.805 --> 00:49:59.467
and non-Europeans in McKinsey?

00:49:59.467 --> 00:50:01.232
And I think in general

00:50:01.232 --> 00:50:04.138
how much focus is there in German seen

00:50:04.138 --> 00:50:08.130
in consulting about international people?

00:50:08.130 --> 00:50:12.133
I will combine it with another
question about the language.

00:50:12.133 --> 00:50:14.466
So the participant asks that

00:50:15.811 --> 00:50:17.055
my Germany is at intermediate level

00:50:17.055 --> 00:50:19.294
and I'm working on it.

00:50:19.294 --> 00:50:22.766
But meanwhile, I do want
to get into consulting.

00:50:22.766 --> 00:50:26.760
What would you advise me on this stage?

00:50:26.760 --> 00:50:27.593
- Well, first of all,

00:50:27.593 --> 00:50:29.113
the quarters of Germans and so on.

00:50:29.113 --> 00:50:31.565
And of course, if you look at the company

00:50:31.565 --> 00:50:33.614
on a global level,

00:50:33.614 --> 00:50:37.864
then I'd say it's representative
of the geographies

00:50:39.127 --> 00:50:40.985
that you are based on.

00:50:40.985 --> 00:50:42.294
The U.S. office, they of course,

00:50:42.294 --> 00:50:43.625
most of the people will be from the U.S.

00:50:43.625 --> 00:50:46.329
and Germany, most people
will be from Germany and Asia

00:50:46.329 --> 00:50:47.476
and specific countries.

00:50:47.476 --> 00:50:50.577
The most people would be
from the local geographies.

00:50:50.577 --> 00:50:52.004
So kind of on a global level

00:50:52.004 --> 00:50:54.418
I'd say it's very much mixed.

00:50:54.418 --> 00:50:55.317
Of course, in the German,

00:50:55.317 --> 00:50:57.949
also the majority of
the people maybe German

00:50:57.949 --> 00:51:00.299
and the main reason for that is that

00:51:00.299 --> 00:51:01.841
you will need to be fluent in Germany

00:51:01.841 --> 00:51:04.042
or at least to kind of
in an advanced level,

00:51:04.042 --> 00:51:05.156
as a German speak out to work

00:51:05.156 --> 00:51:07.863
in the German office of consulting firms.

00:51:07.863 --> 00:51:09.962
And again, now if you
maybe working IT consulting

00:51:09.962 --> 00:51:12.728
where maybe you're also not
that client facing and so on,

00:51:12.728 --> 00:51:13.964
there might be some differences,

00:51:13.964 --> 00:51:16.655
but at least for the
big MBB consulting firms

00:51:16.655 --> 00:51:18.375
from McKinsey, BCG, Bain or so

00:51:18.375 --> 00:51:19.984
you can assume that then yes,

00:51:19.984 --> 00:51:21.738
you will need to speak the language.

00:51:21.738 --> 00:51:22.698
So what can you do

00:51:22.698 --> 00:51:25.407
if you do not have that
level of proficiency

00:51:25.407 --> 00:51:27.107
in the German language,

00:51:27.107 --> 00:51:30.018
but you wanna still work in Germany?

00:51:30.018 --> 00:51:31.124
I mean, frankly, I believe

00:51:31.124 --> 00:51:32.324
the only thing, at least

00:51:32.324 --> 00:51:35.601
again, if you now look at
the MBB consulting firms,

00:51:35.601 --> 00:51:38.020
the only roles that you
might be then open for

00:51:38.020 --> 00:51:39.790
are more kind of the back office role,

00:51:39.790 --> 00:51:41.120
the analytics teams,

00:51:41.120 --> 00:51:42.647
the support teams,

00:51:42.647 --> 00:51:44.654
and there can be some exciting
opportunities as well,

00:51:44.654 --> 00:51:47.878
so especially if you have some IT skills,

00:51:47.878 --> 00:51:49.715
there are always statistics teams like,

00:51:49.715 --> 00:51:52.094
data modeling, data science teams

00:51:52.094 --> 00:51:54.060
that then support the consultants

00:51:54.060 --> 00:51:55.247
from the background.

00:51:55.247 --> 00:51:57.750
And then yes, maybe you
would also then a few days

00:51:57.750 --> 00:51:59.482
join the team on the ground of the client

00:51:59.482 --> 00:52:02.218
to help them every bit (mumbles)
together with somebody.

00:52:02.218 --> 00:52:04.191
And then you just kind of
support them in these ways.

00:52:04.191 --> 00:52:05.408
There then also research teams,

00:52:05.408 --> 00:52:08.867
so McKinsey (mumbles) RNI
research and information.

00:52:08.867 --> 00:52:12.199
So, whenever a consultant
needs to collect information

00:52:12.199 --> 00:52:13.766
from reports or whatever,

00:52:13.766 --> 00:52:15.020
then there are these research guys,

00:52:15.020 --> 00:52:16.652
they can then draw on

00:52:16.652 --> 00:52:18.707
to then conduct these analysis for them.

00:52:18.707 --> 00:52:20.355
But the truth, also see us,

00:52:20.355 --> 00:52:22.431
even though I'd say these
are interesting jobs

00:52:22.431 --> 00:52:25.267
and they're also very fairly compensated,

00:52:25.267 --> 00:52:30.267
it's not what you are envisioning
if you work in consulting.

00:52:30.340 --> 00:52:34.140
It's not really the life of
the client facing or tightened,

00:52:34.140 --> 00:52:34.973
and also,

00:52:36.255 --> 00:52:37.255
in the firm.

00:52:38.188 --> 00:52:42.879
So even though this is
kind of a constant topic,

00:52:42.879 --> 00:52:43.902
I'd say the culture

00:52:43.902 --> 00:52:44.735
even though people try lots

00:52:44.735 --> 00:52:48.006
to now create a class system in the firm,

00:52:48.006 --> 00:52:49.685
but you man, people are above the other

00:52:49.685 --> 00:52:50.960
or something like that.

00:52:50.960 --> 00:52:52.786
That's just the fact that,

00:52:52.786 --> 00:52:56.416
there's one level with the
client facing consultants.

00:52:56.416 --> 00:52:58.630
And then there's the other
level with all the rest,

00:52:58.630 --> 00:53:00.257
all the people supporting them.

00:53:00.257 --> 00:53:02.515
And I think people do a very good job

00:53:02.515 --> 00:53:04.679
of trying to not make
these people feel bad

00:53:04.679 --> 00:53:05.944
and trying to encourage them,

00:53:05.944 --> 00:53:07.995
and to have a great and
inclusive firm environment.

00:53:07.995 --> 00:53:09.353
And I believe in companies like McKinsey,

00:53:09.353 --> 00:53:11.461
this works really well.

00:53:11.461 --> 00:53:13.251
But I would ask myself,

00:53:13.251 --> 00:53:14.712
but this is really the experience

00:53:14.712 --> 00:53:17.202
that you want to have,

00:53:17.202 --> 00:53:20.098
or whether there are maybe
some other opportunities

00:53:20.098 --> 00:53:21.617
that maybe suit your profile,

00:53:21.617 --> 00:53:23.551
suit your goals to be better.

00:53:23.551 --> 00:53:25.135
Because also the truth is by,

00:53:25.135 --> 00:53:26.793
it does happen.

00:53:26.793 --> 00:53:28.176
Sometimes people make the switch.

00:53:28.176 --> 00:53:30.058
So I know also a couple
of people who did that

00:53:30.058 --> 00:53:32.325
from these more back office research roads

00:53:32.325 --> 00:53:34.533
into then the client
facing consulting role.

00:53:34.533 --> 00:53:36.408
So it's not impossible,

00:53:36.408 --> 00:53:38.421
but I'd say it's very rare,

00:53:38.421 --> 00:53:40.213
and rather than usually.

00:53:40.213 --> 00:53:42.953
So this is not something
you should count on

00:53:42.953 --> 00:53:44.344
of then doing the switch.

00:53:44.344 --> 00:53:46.316
It's just very uncommon.

00:53:46.316 --> 00:53:47.258
There are some examples,

00:53:47.258 --> 00:53:48.580
but it's just very uncommon

00:53:48.580 --> 00:53:50.084
and you should bid your career

00:53:50.084 --> 00:53:51.110
based on the idea.

00:53:51.110 --> 00:53:52.958
Then after some years you
just do the transition.

00:53:52.958 --> 00:53:56.458
So this is not something you can count on.

00:53:59.963 --> 00:54:01.478
- Thanks.

00:54:01.478 --> 00:54:03.811
Just checking the questions.

00:54:05.888 --> 00:54:07.614
Well, very quick note.

00:54:07.614 --> 00:54:10.126
I mean, we are almost,

00:54:10.126 --> 00:54:11.847
we're already over the time.

00:54:11.847 --> 00:54:15.888
Still I will try to
incorporate all the questions.

00:54:15.888 --> 00:54:16.721
- Sure.

00:54:29.294 --> 00:54:34.294
I think I will take one from
the pre-submitted questions

00:54:34.302 --> 00:54:35.682
and that is,

00:54:35.682 --> 00:54:38.408
are you part of McKinsey alumni network?

00:54:38.408 --> 00:54:43.241
And could you share what role
does it play after the exit?

00:54:44.239 --> 00:54:46.026
- Yes, I'm part of the alumni network,

00:54:46.026 --> 00:54:48.800
and I think everybody
has to leave the farm

00:54:48.800 --> 00:54:50.435
in good standing.

00:54:50.435 --> 00:54:51.915
So yes,

00:54:51.915 --> 00:54:54.195
frankly, I haven't used it that much yet

00:54:54.195 --> 00:54:55.942
because I also directly got a new job.

00:54:55.942 --> 00:54:57.500
I believe that's kind of a panto

00:54:57.500 --> 00:54:59.920
where you then can get in
touch with other alumni,

00:54:59.920 --> 00:55:01.503
and then there are also regular events

00:55:01.503 --> 00:55:04.039
that on the alumni meet.

00:55:04.039 --> 00:55:06.928
So frankly, I haven't
really made use of that.

00:55:06.928 --> 00:55:09.653
So I believe it can be helpful.

00:55:09.653 --> 00:55:10.928
It's probably not now,

00:55:10.928 --> 00:55:12.962
a life changing experience
of being in this.

00:55:12.962 --> 00:55:13.795
Alumni network,

00:55:13.795 --> 00:55:16.274
but of course, there are many alumni

00:55:16.274 --> 00:55:17.818
now who work in inferential roads

00:55:17.818 --> 00:55:19.727
around the world and lots of companies,

00:55:19.727 --> 00:55:21.269
and are reaching out to them

00:55:21.269 --> 00:55:23.646
via either we are the official network

00:55:23.646 --> 00:55:24.611
or via LinkedIn,

00:55:24.611 --> 00:55:26.173
or some other ways.

00:55:26.173 --> 00:55:30.237
I believe of course, it's
helpful for you in the long-run.

00:55:30.237 --> 00:55:31.070
But I trust,

00:55:31.070 --> 00:55:34.820
this is similar with
other big firms as well.

00:55:36.648 --> 00:55:39.899
- You're also targeted this
during earlier questions

00:55:39.899 --> 00:55:42.268
that it's a very high performing job

00:55:42.268 --> 00:55:44.521
and it takes a lot of time.

00:55:44.521 --> 00:55:47.568
So however, it also depends
on country-to-country

00:55:47.568 --> 00:55:48.663
and office-to-office,

00:55:48.663 --> 00:55:50.544
the culture and working hours.

00:55:50.544 --> 00:55:53.923
So how is it exactly in Germany?

00:55:53.923 --> 00:55:57.317
- Yeah, I mean, there are lots
of stereotypes around that.

00:55:57.317 --> 00:55:59.372
I mean, the stereotype
is that the German office

00:55:59.372 --> 00:56:00.596
is one of the worst offices

00:56:00.596 --> 00:56:04.004
in terms of work-life balance--

00:56:04.004 --> 00:56:05.573
- Germany love to work.

00:56:05.573 --> 00:56:06.543
(laughs)

00:56:06.543 --> 00:56:07.469
- That's to work,

00:56:07.469 --> 00:56:08.302
probably only toped

00:56:08.302 --> 00:56:10.474
in the negative sense
by the Italian office.

00:56:10.474 --> 00:56:13.423
So the Italian office is
considered even worse,

00:56:13.423 --> 00:56:15.918
and also the Japan office
is considered very bad.

00:56:15.918 --> 00:56:18.887
I mean, these are probably
like the top three.

00:56:18.887 --> 00:56:20.547
And then on the other side,

00:56:20.547 --> 00:56:22.256
people who are considered
to be very chilled

00:56:22.256 --> 00:56:23.692
is all the Nordics,

00:56:23.692 --> 00:56:24.885
so if you work in the Nordics,

00:56:24.885 --> 00:56:26.796
this is considered very laid back,

00:56:26.796 --> 00:56:28.800
but also the Dutch office, for instance,

00:56:28.800 --> 00:56:30.871
this is to be considered quite chill.

00:56:30.871 --> 00:56:35.345
And then the rest is
somewhere in the middle.

00:56:35.345 --> 00:56:36.799
I mean, probably in terms of direction,

00:56:36.799 --> 00:56:37.882
this is true.

00:56:39.306 --> 00:56:41.554
But again, it differs a
lot project-by-project

00:56:41.554 --> 00:56:44.437
and also the people that you work with.

00:56:44.437 --> 00:56:46.397
Some projects just will
make you work longer

00:56:46.397 --> 00:56:48.656
than other projects just
because how the setup is,

00:56:48.656 --> 00:56:50.631
just how the requirements are.

00:56:50.631 --> 00:56:52.159
And they're also just some teams,

00:56:52.159 --> 00:56:54.390
some project leads who are more sensitive

00:56:54.390 --> 00:56:56.321
to work-life balance than others.

00:56:56.321 --> 00:56:57.212
And here again,

00:56:57.212 --> 00:56:58.249
this is all a bit also something

00:56:58.249 --> 00:57:00.251
that you will learn over time

00:57:00.251 --> 00:57:01.236
to kind of find the people

00:57:01.236 --> 00:57:03.366
that you like to work with.

00:57:03.366 --> 00:57:06.203
And then maybe these
can also be the people,

00:57:06.203 --> 00:57:09.423
do not push you until
after midnight every night.

00:57:09.423 --> 00:57:12.677
Because it just can be
super tired afterward.

00:57:12.677 --> 00:57:13.511
So, but yes,

00:57:13.511 --> 00:57:16.802
the truth is that you
will work much longer

00:57:16.802 --> 00:57:18.208
than many other jobs.

00:57:18.208 --> 00:57:19.341
The positive side of that,

00:57:19.341 --> 00:57:21.906
and this is also true
for the German office,

00:57:21.906 --> 00:57:23.188
at least at McKinsey what I experienced

00:57:23.188 --> 00:57:26.938
is that the weekends
are very well respected.

00:57:27.898 --> 00:57:29.268
And this is I believe a big difference too

00:57:29.268 --> 00:57:30.509
for instance banking.

00:57:30.509 --> 00:57:33.052
So if you work in
Frankfurt, in the MesseTurm

00:57:33.052 --> 00:57:34.133
with Goldman Sachs or whatever

00:57:34.133 --> 00:57:36.715
you can expect to be in
the office every Saturday,

00:57:36.715 --> 00:57:37.989
every Sunday,

00:57:37.989 --> 00:57:39.289
weekends every week.

00:57:39.289 --> 00:57:43.361
This is not the case with
the big consulting firms.

00:57:43.361 --> 00:57:44.318
So I mean, yes,

00:57:44.318 --> 00:57:46.234
there's really a hard business need.

00:57:46.234 --> 00:57:47.777
And it's now very, very urgent,

00:57:47.777 --> 00:57:50.818
then yes, you will be asked
to work on the weekend.

00:57:50.818 --> 00:57:52.530
And then this would be
also expected from you,

00:57:52.530 --> 00:57:54.890
for probably for me this happens

00:57:54.890 --> 00:57:56.451
five, six, seven times or so

00:57:56.451 --> 00:57:58.947
in the years I've been at McKinsey.

00:57:58.947 --> 00:58:00.014
So it does happen,

00:58:00.014 --> 00:58:02.142
but not super frequently.

00:58:02.142 --> 00:58:04.100
And so we can solve very well respected

00:58:04.100 --> 00:58:07.492
and I believe it this is a big (mumbles).

00:58:07.492 --> 00:58:08.620
- Great.

00:58:08.620 --> 00:58:10.012
Adhil is still raising hands.

00:58:10.012 --> 00:58:11.431
So I will allow him to talk.

00:58:11.431 --> 00:58:12.314
- Sure.

00:58:12.314 --> 00:58:14.064
- Adhil, here you go.

00:58:15.992 --> 00:58:18.075
- [Adhil] My question is,

00:58:19.632 --> 00:58:21.757
does an MBA add any value to somebody

00:58:21.757 --> 00:58:23.624
who wants to enter consulting?

00:58:23.624 --> 00:58:25.289
And my second question is,

00:58:25.289 --> 00:58:28.110
it possible to transition from consulting

00:58:28.110 --> 00:58:29.392
to investment banking

00:58:29.392 --> 00:58:31.077
after getting three to
four years of experience

00:58:31.077 --> 00:58:32.244
in consulting?

00:58:34.880 --> 00:58:36.725
- So the first question,

00:58:36.725 --> 00:58:38.930
I mean, yes, an MBA (mumbles)

00:58:38.930 --> 00:58:40.596
or no, let's put it differently.

00:58:40.596 --> 00:58:43.010
I'd say consulting firms
hire a lot of MBAs.

00:58:43.010 --> 00:58:43.969
This is effect,

00:58:43.969 --> 00:58:46.649
especially if did an MBA

00:58:46.649 --> 00:58:49.672
at one of the big well
respective programs,

00:58:49.672 --> 00:58:52.313
you have a good chance of them
getting hired by consulting.

00:58:52.313 --> 00:58:54.576
I mean, I did the incite MBA in France,

00:58:54.576 --> 00:58:57.089
and then also on the Singapore compost.

00:58:57.089 --> 00:58:59.011
And this is one of the MBA programs

00:58:59.011 --> 00:59:01.313
that is very close to consulting firms.

00:59:01.313 --> 00:59:03.707
So a lot of people then join consulting.

00:59:03.707 --> 00:59:05.199
Also a lot of people
with other backgrounds

00:59:05.199 --> 00:59:07.212
before with industry background,

00:59:07.212 --> 00:59:08.580
corporate backgrounds

00:59:08.580 --> 00:59:11.052
later joined MBB consulting firms.

00:59:11.052 --> 00:59:14.970
So this is for sure a
path that you can take.

00:59:14.970 --> 00:59:15.867
But this is not to say

00:59:15.867 --> 00:59:18.230
that you need to have an
MBA to get into consulting,

00:59:18.230 --> 00:59:19.275
especially in Germany,

00:59:19.275 --> 00:59:21.994
I'd say that MBAs are still

00:59:21.994 --> 00:59:22.827
not that popular.

00:59:22.827 --> 00:59:23.710
So for instance,

00:59:23.710 --> 00:59:25.484
doing a PhD in Germany
is much more popular

00:59:25.484 --> 00:59:26.984
than doing an MBA.

00:59:29.124 --> 00:59:30.707
And it's not at all a must have criteria

00:59:30.707 --> 00:59:33.254
to condition into the consulting role.

00:59:33.254 --> 00:59:36.015
So of course, now it
depends on your profile.

00:59:36.015 --> 00:59:37.114
For some profiles, it can really help,

00:59:37.114 --> 00:59:41.029
maybe you have a more
specific corporate role,

00:59:41.029 --> 00:59:42.682
kind of more technical role

00:59:42.682 --> 00:59:44.022
of some years of experience

00:59:44.022 --> 00:59:45.645
in a big corporate or something.

00:59:45.645 --> 00:59:47.707
Then from that, maybe it can be a bit hard

00:59:47.707 --> 00:59:50.272
to not directly position into
strategy consulting firm.

00:59:50.272 --> 00:59:51.705
And then an MBA can really help you

00:59:51.705 --> 00:59:54.165
to make it more interesting to employers,

00:59:54.165 --> 00:59:56.916
to maybe also broaden
all your horizon a bit.

00:59:56.916 --> 00:59:58.310
And then for these kinds of profiles

00:59:58.310 --> 01:00:01.643
that can be very helpful to have an MBA.

01:00:03.164 --> 01:00:04.900
Now you need to remind
me of the second part

01:00:04.900 --> 01:00:06.733
of the question again.

01:00:09.240 --> 01:00:10.073
- Second part of the question

01:00:10.073 --> 01:00:14.551
was I think, can you transition
into investment banking

01:00:14.551 --> 01:00:15.459
from consulting after?

01:00:15.459 --> 01:00:16.370
- Yeah.

01:00:16.370 --> 01:00:17.316
So yes, this is possible.

01:00:17.316 --> 01:00:18.171
And I've also seen,

01:00:18.171 --> 01:00:21.254
I mean, I know of
different people who did.

01:00:21.254 --> 01:00:22.762
Here, of course, (mumbles)

01:00:22.762 --> 01:00:25.315
that then during your consulting time,

01:00:25.315 --> 01:00:26.848
you did relevant projects.

01:00:26.848 --> 01:00:29.356
So for instance, you
did lots of udiligences

01:00:29.356 --> 01:00:32.404
you worked maybe close
to M&amp;A things and so on,

01:00:32.404 --> 01:00:33.856
but of course you need to realize

01:00:33.856 --> 01:00:35.744
that your udiligences

01:00:35.744 --> 01:00:38.435
probably are the worst projects out there

01:00:38.435 --> 01:00:40.029
in terms of work life balance.

01:00:40.029 --> 01:00:40.862
So, because the diligence is off

01:00:40.862 --> 01:00:44.279
not only two weeks,
three weeks, four weeks,

01:00:44.279 --> 01:00:45.987
it's a very short amount of time.

01:00:45.987 --> 01:00:48.025
You work together with
lots of other professionals

01:00:48.025 --> 01:00:49.938
of private equity and so on.

01:00:49.938 --> 01:00:53.005
And I mean, I knew one
guy who was just crazy.

01:00:53.005 --> 01:00:54.689
I mean, he was literally crazy,

01:00:54.689 --> 01:00:57.946
and he did one due
diligence after each other

01:00:57.946 --> 01:00:59.745
and like for a year or two years or so.

01:00:59.745 --> 01:01:02.001
I mean, if this is what you want to do,

01:01:02.001 --> 01:01:04.153
then, feel free,

01:01:04.153 --> 01:01:06.199
but you just need to realize

01:01:06.199 --> 01:01:08.691
that this is the probably
the type of stuff

01:01:08.691 --> 01:01:10.082
that investment firms,

01:01:10.082 --> 01:01:11.867
investment banking firms
would be interested in,

01:01:11.867 --> 01:01:13.846
but this is the stuff that really is one

01:01:13.846 --> 01:01:16.086
of the toughest stuff to do.

01:01:16.086 --> 01:01:17.948
And of course that's also middle way.

01:01:17.948 --> 01:01:18.874
And maybe you do that occasionally,

01:01:18.874 --> 01:01:20.719
and then you do that
for two or three weeks

01:01:20.719 --> 01:01:22.770
and then you go do more normal projects

01:01:22.770 --> 01:01:26.203
and then you do the diligence this again.

01:01:26.203 --> 01:01:28.030
And of course, also
some other project work

01:01:28.030 --> 01:01:29.968
in the corporate finance road and so on

01:01:29.968 --> 01:01:31.223
where maybe it's not like the diligence

01:01:31.223 --> 01:01:32.870
all the time can also
be interesting to banks,

01:01:32.870 --> 01:01:34.760
but just be aware that
probably then you need

01:01:34.760 --> 01:01:36.943
